From f1fb9cb986972c240a9158710914ffe1c8911443 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: wray-lee <wray-lee@outlook.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 17:00:04 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] feat(slack): handle the single Slack slash command over
 Socket Mode

Slack delivers a slash command over Socket Mode as a `slash_commands`
envelope, which the socket loop acked and silently dropped (it only
processed `events_api`). Registering one OpenClaw-style command and
typing "/nullclaw /stop" therefore did nothing.

- Add channels.slack.accounts.<id>.slash_command { enabled, name }
  (default disabled; name "nullclaw", compared without the leading "/"
  and case-insensitively).
- In handleSocketPayload, after the ack (Slack's 3s budget), route
  `slash_commands` envelopes to handleSlashCommand: match the command
  name, apply the same DM/group allowlist gates as a normal message, then
  publish payload.text as a top-level message in payload.channel_id.
  Everything downstream is unchanged, so /stop reaches the same interrupt
  path as a typed "/stop".
- A stop command is always top-level and carries no thread context, so
  the enqueue-time stop fast-path now interrupts the routed session and
  any active `:thread:` child of it: `/nullclaw /stop` also interrupts a
  turn running inside a thread under that channel.
- Ignore retried slash_commands envelopes (retry_attempt > 0) so a failed
  ack cannot run the command twice; bound the response_url POST so a slow
  reply cannot stall the socket read loop.
- A slash command is an explicit, app-directed invocation, so it counts
  as a mention for group_policy=mention_only; empty text replies with
  usage via response_url; an unauthorized sender gets an ephemeral
  refusal; a command name that does not match the configured name is
  logged and answered with an ephemeral notice instead of being silent.
- Extract the inbound-publish tail of processHistoryMessage into
  publishInboundMessage so both paths derive the same chat_id/session_key.
- Tests: name match, publish, /stop through the daemon stop fast-path for
  both the channel session and a busy thread session, unknown name, auth
  refusal, empty text, envelope ack-before-dispatch, retry suppression,
  response-body encoding, config parse/defaults.
- Docs (en/zh).
